-- title:
--   Prime-to-p toric points lie in the monodromy toric part
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $N_0$ and $p$ with $N_0 \neq 0$, $p$ prime and $p \nmid N_0$, and a valuation subring $A$ of a fixed algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ of $\mathbf Q$ lying over $p$, in the sense that the image of $p$ lies in the nonunits of $A$. Let $\Lambda$ be level data for $(N_0,p,A)$, that is, a structure morphism $\mathrm{Spec}\,A \to \mathrm{base}\,p$ compatible with the generic point, a scheme $X$ over $\mathrm{base}\,p$ with a relative group law, and bijections identifying $\mathrm{JZero}\,N_0 = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the level-$N_0$ modular function field over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ with the generic sections of $X$, and the corresponding group over the residue field of $A$ with the sections over the residue point; assume $\Lambda$ is Jacobian, i.e. $X$ satisfies the abelian-scheme property bundle, its group law is commutative, both point bijections are additive, the generic one is Galois-equivariant, reduction of points agrees modulo the maximal ideal when the relevant inputs hold, and every Hecke operator is realised by an endomorphism of $X$ over $\mathrm{base}\,p$. Let $O$ be a Néron object at $p$ over these data: a smooth, separated, locally of finite type, quasi-compact, surjective group scheme $G \to \mathrm{base}\,p$ with preconnected fibres and commutative relative group law, a bijection of $\mathrm{JZero}(N_0p)$ with the generic sections that is additive, Galois-equivariant and Hecke-equivariant, flat surjective multiplication-by-$n$ maps, proper generic fibre, together with its numerical invariants (toric rank, widths, toric and finite point subgroups). Assume the unipotence hypothesis $\mathrm{hIU}$: for every $m$ coprime to $p$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbf Q$, viewed inside $\mathrm{Aut}(\overline{\mathbf Q}/\mathbf Q)$ as the image of the inertia subgroup under the inclusion of the decomposition subgroup, and every $m$-torsion point $x$ of $\mathrm{JZero}(N_0p)$, one has $\sigma \cdot x - x \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,m$. Then, for every $m$ coprime to $p$, with $\mathrm{JZero}(N_0p)$ carrying the Hecke-algebra module structure `heckeModuleBar (N₀ * p)`, the subgroup $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,m$ — the subgroup generated by the image of the family of toric points of $O$ at level $m$, and $\bot$ if $m = 0$ — is contained in the additive subgroup underlying $\mathrm{toricMonodromyPart}$ of $p$ and the inertia subgroup of $A$, namely the Hecke-submodule of $\mathrm{JZero}(N_0p)$ spanned by the differences $\sigma \cdot x - x$ with $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup and $x$ killed by some positive integer coprime to $p$.
--
--   This is the inclusion of the toric part of the prime-to-$p$ torsion of $J_0(N_0p)$ into the part cut out by inertial monodromy at a place above $p$, in the form used in Ribet's analysis of the reduction of $J_0(N_0p)$ at $p$. It is invoked in the comparison of the toric and finite point subgroups and in the identification of the action of a Frobenius element on toric points with the action of a Hecke operator.
